WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.620
Imagine an economy where 94% of the participants are actively losing money.

00:08.240 --> 00:09.540
Yeah, that sounds like a total disaster.

00:10.020 --> 00:13.460
Right. But the system isn't a scam. It's completely legal.

00:13.880 --> 00:18.380
And the platform running it is actually reporting record-breaking billions in profit.

00:18.840 --> 00:20.600
Which is just wild to think about.

00:20.940 --> 00:23.920
It really is. So welcome to The Deep Dive.

00:24.520 --> 00:29.140
Today, our mission is to uncover what is basically the ultimate economic illusion for you.

00:29.140 --> 00:41.480
I mean, we usually assume that if a digital platform, say a massive cloud provider or an app store, is generating staggering revenue, the people paying to use it must also be thriving.

00:41.980 --> 00:47.160
Exactly. We treat platform revenue like this, I don't know, this economic x-ray of shared prosperity.

00:47.840 --> 00:51.240
But the math tells a much darker, much more fascinating story.

00:51.880 --> 00:54.840
It's a massive blind spot in how we evaluate tech economies.

00:54.840 --> 01:00.240
We see the towering success of the host and, you know, we project that success onto the users.

01:00.940 --> 01:08.600
But the underlying architecture of these systems is built to completely decouple the platform's financial health from the user's actual outcomes.

01:09.000 --> 01:11.120
Decouple is the perfect word for it.

01:11.800 --> 01:14.700
And that decoupling is exactly what we're digging into today.

01:14.700 --> 01:29.800
We are going to uncover how platforms that sell access to experimentation, like software subscriptions or generative AI, can be wildly profitable, even when the typical person using them is deep in the red.

01:30.520 --> 01:37.340
And we are basing this entire conversation on a really incredible, deeply analytical paper from October 2026.

01:37.340 --> 01:45.740
Yeah. It's by the independent researcher, Flyxion, titled Revenue Without Returns, Platform Income Under Negative Financial Returns.

01:46.040 --> 01:54.120
This paper is just a rigorous mathematical teardown of, well, our most comforting assumptions about commercial symbiosis.

01:54.120 --> 02:10.640
So we're going to explore how this dynamic works under the hood for you, why users keep cheerfully signing up to lose money, and how this specific architecture is fundamentally shaping the massive AI language model industry right now.

02:10.860 --> 02:13.540
It's basically eating the internet as we speak.

02:13.660 --> 02:14.300
It really is.

02:14.700 --> 02:18.140
So let's start by looking at the core machinery of this illusion.

02:18.140 --> 02:25.860
If I open a brick-and-mortar restaurant and my customers universally hate the food, my revenue collapses.

02:26.180 --> 02:29.060
Right. The feedback loop is immediate. You go out of business.

02:29.400 --> 02:29.800
Exactly.

02:30.620 --> 02:37.500
So why is it structurally different for a digital platform selling access to a software suite or like an advertising network?

02:38.160 --> 02:41.720
The divergence begins with the fundamental structure of the transaction itself.

02:41.980 --> 02:45.140
The paper introduces what it calls the minimal model.

02:45.260 --> 02:46.120
The minimal model.

02:46.120 --> 02:50.040
Yeah. And it isolates the wedge between the buyer and the seller.

02:50.640 --> 02:53.920
I mean, in a traditional service business, you pay for a guaranteed outcome.

02:54.140 --> 02:55.660
By getting a meal at a restaurant.

02:55.820 --> 02:59.140
Right. But in these platforms, you are paying for an attempt.

02:59.580 --> 03:02.360
The platform charges a fixed fee just to swing the bat.

03:03.040 --> 03:03.480
Oh, I see.

03:03.660 --> 03:09.280
But the participant, you know, they only get a financial return based on the actual outcome of that swing.

03:09.480 --> 03:13.460
So the platform is selling the attempt completely insulated from the outcome.

03:13.460 --> 03:21.200
So let's put some hard numbers to this because the paper provides a specific hypothetical scenario that makes the math impossible to ignore.

03:21.200 --> 03:22.840
The numbers really bring it home.

03:22.840 --> 03:25.280
They do. Imagine a digital platform.

03:25.700 --> 03:29.080
Maybe it's a service that lets you easily build and launch mobile games.

03:29.540 --> 03:32.000
And the platform has 10,000 projects running on it.

03:32.420 --> 03:37.660
So that's 10,000 developers who have all decided to take a shot at building the next viral hit.

03:37.660 --> 03:43.800
Exactly. Now, the platform charges a flat fee of $200 for this service. That is their price.

03:44.300 --> 03:46.100
Right. $200 just to enter the room.

03:46.400 --> 03:52.440
Yep. And the platform's internal cost to provide the server space and tools is, let's say, $80 per user.

03:52.840 --> 03:59.840
Okay. So that leaves the platform with a margin of $120 on every single one of those 10,000 projects.

03:59.920 --> 04:00.560
Every single one.

04:00.560 --> 04:10.880
So if we pause right there and look solely at the platform's balance sheet, they are pulling in $2 million in total revenue against $800,000 in costs.

04:11.260 --> 04:15.260
Which means they have a contribution margin of $1.2 million.

04:15.720 --> 04:16.280
Massive.

04:16.880 --> 04:21.360
Right. From the perspective of a Silicon Valley investor, this company is a total rocket ship.

04:21.460 --> 04:24.440
I mean, they are printing money on every new user they acquire.

04:24.440 --> 04:28.460
But now we have to flip the perspective to the developers paying that $200.

04:29.660 --> 04:34.720
The paper sets the probability of a project actually succeeding at just 6%.

04:34.720 --> 04:37.200
And honestly, 6% is entirely realistic.

04:37.500 --> 04:43.260
It's perhaps even generous for a highly experimental venture like mobile games or, you know, independent content creators.

04:43.640 --> 04:44.140
Very generous.

04:44.900 --> 04:47.980
So if you are in that lucky 6%, you hit a home run.

04:49.360 --> 04:53.740
The paper sets the gross return for a successful project at $1,500.

04:54.440 --> 04:55.860
That's a huge payout.

04:56.080 --> 04:56.320
It is.

04:56.780 --> 05:00.520
I want to slow down and really map out what happens to the money in this digital room.

05:01.460 --> 05:03.060
We have 10,000 people, right?

05:03.080 --> 05:04.020
10,000 people, yeah.

05:04.340 --> 05:06.680
They each hand the platform a $200 entry fee.

05:07.620 --> 05:11.080
The platform instantly pockets a guarantee cut of $1.2 million.

05:12.640 --> 05:16.020
Then the platform effectively spins a massive roulette wheel.

05:16.400 --> 05:17.540
That's exactly what it is.

05:18.120 --> 05:22.800
And out of the 10,000 people standing in that room, only 600 of them hit the winning number.

05:23.060 --> 05:23.820
Just 600.

05:23.820 --> 05:24.860
Just 600.

05:25.440 --> 05:25.700
Right.

05:26.100 --> 05:28.660
Those 600 developers take home $1,500.

05:29.400 --> 05:33.520
Subtracting their initial $200 fee, they clear a pure profit of $1,300.

05:34.260 --> 05:36.380
And those 600 people are ecstatic.

05:37.060 --> 05:39.560
I mean, they're the ones getting interviewed on podcasts.

05:39.960 --> 05:40.240
Oh, yeah.

05:40.460 --> 05:45.480
They are writing massive threads on social media about how they ground their way to the top.

05:46.020 --> 05:51.760
They're the glowing testimonials on the platform's front page, acting as like living proof that the system works.

05:51.760 --> 05:54.040
Because they are the highly visible signal.

05:54.040 --> 06:01.300
But the noise, the massive silent reality, is the other 9,400 people in that room.

06:01.300 --> 06:02.240
The people who lost.

06:02.620 --> 06:03.100
Exactly.

06:03.320 --> 06:05.420
The roulette wheel didn't land on their number.

06:05.840 --> 06:10.540
They walk out with zero return, having permanently lost their $200 entry fee.

06:10.800 --> 06:12.900
This brings us to the core accounting paradox.

06:13.220 --> 06:20.540
Because if we look at the expected return for the average user, we have to multiply their 6% chance of winning by the $1,500 payout.

06:20.540 --> 06:22.520
Right, which comes out to $90.

06:23.080 --> 06:23.480
$90.

06:24.300 --> 06:29.460
The expected gross return is $90, but the fee to enter the room is $200.

06:30.180 --> 06:30.580
Oh, wow.

06:30.760 --> 06:37.220
So this means the mathematically expected profit for anyone walking through that door is negative $110.

06:37.940 --> 06:41.000
The average person is mathematically guaranteed to bleed money.

06:41.820 --> 06:44.420
I mean, this feels like the California gold rush all over again, doesn't it?

06:44.480 --> 06:45.980
It's the exact same mechanic, yeah.

06:45.980 --> 06:52.980
The people selling the pickaxes and the denim jeans were getting incredibly wealthy on every single swing of a pickaxe,

06:53.680 --> 06:56.280
regardless of whether there was actually any gold in the river.

06:56.580 --> 07:03.980
And the shovel seller can point to the one miner who found a massive nugget and use them as a walking billboard to sell 10,000 more shovels.

07:04.860 --> 07:06.100
But here's my question.

07:06.640 --> 07:10.660
Doesn't the shovel seller eventually run out of customers if everyone keeps busting?

07:11.140 --> 07:14.360
Well, this leads us to the concept of risk aggregation.

07:14.360 --> 07:20.900
The platform aggregates the micro-risks of the individuals into a predictable macro-revenue stream.

07:21.440 --> 07:22.640
Okay, unpack that for me.

07:23.100 --> 07:27.880
We often hear tech executives claim that their platform absorbs risk through diversification.

07:28.340 --> 07:33.900
But this model proves that the platform doesn't bear the risk of individual project failure at all.

07:34.280 --> 07:35.100
They get paid up front.

07:35.560 --> 07:35.780
Right.

07:35.880 --> 07:39.100
Their only real risk is that people stop showing up to buy shovels.

07:39.100 --> 07:39.700
Exactly.

07:40.460 --> 07:45.840
The platform's vulnerability is entirely tied to overall user volume and market shifts over time.

07:46.340 --> 07:48.800
So diversification doesn't protect them.

07:48.800 --> 07:53.920
Diversification across 10,000 users just smooths out the statistical noise.

07:53.920 --> 07:56.460
It makes the revenue predictable month over month.

07:56.620 --> 08:00.800
But it offers zero protection against a correlated industry shock.

08:01.580 --> 08:01.840
Like what?

08:02.280 --> 08:03.240
Like a new regulation.

08:03.840 --> 08:07.780
Yeah, like a new regulatory environment that makes mobile gaming unprofitable.

08:08.020 --> 08:11.200
Or, you know, a competitor launching a shovel that only costs $50.

08:11.200 --> 08:16.900
The platform is selling a lottery ticket insulated from the scratching of the ticket itself.

08:17.320 --> 08:18.800
I want to push on that lottery analogy.

08:19.300 --> 08:26.900
Because if the average return is negative $110, the reality for the typical person is actually much worse, right?

08:27.640 --> 08:29.500
Oh, significantly worse.

08:29.920 --> 08:35.600
There is a massive psychological trick happening with how we report success in these ecosystems.

08:36.080 --> 08:39.780
The paper insists on separating three distinct metrics, doesn't it?

08:39.780 --> 08:44.980
Yes, anyone analyzing these platforms must separate three distinct quantities.

08:45.640 --> 08:50.460
The mean outcome, the typical outcome, and the platform's operating profit.

08:51.120 --> 08:53.700
So the mean, the typical, and the profit.

08:54.480 --> 09:00.960
Conflating the mean, the average, with the typical experience is the source of endless confusion in economic reporting.

09:01.440 --> 09:03.040
Because averages hide the extremes.

09:03.040 --> 09:10.940
I mean, if a billionaire walks into a crowded dive bar, the average net worth of everyone in that room suddenly skyrockets to $50 million.

09:11.500 --> 09:14.260
Right. Technically true, but highly misleading.

09:14.720 --> 09:20.540
Exactly. If I talk to the bartender or the person sitting next to me, their financial reality hasn't changed a single cent.

09:20.540 --> 09:25.040
That's why we have to look at the typical outcome, which is represented by the median.

09:25.220 --> 09:25.680
The median.

09:25.860 --> 09:26.060
Right.

09:26.700 --> 09:32.560
In environments of experimentation, success isn't distributed like a bell curve where most people land somewhere in the middle.

09:32.820 --> 09:35.020
It is a heavy-tailed distribution.

09:35.300 --> 09:42.440
Meaning the outcomes are heavily skewed toward a tiny minority of massive winners, while the vast majority experience binary failure.

09:42.440 --> 09:45.200
Let's apply that to our room of 10,000 developers.

09:45.720 --> 09:55.720
If I line them all up from the worst outcome to the best outcome, and I grab the person standing exactly in the middle, the 5,000th person, what does their balance sheet look like?

09:56.240 --> 10:00.740
Well, that person is in the group of 9,400 users who made absolutely nothing.

10:01.160 --> 10:01.540
Exactly.

10:02.140 --> 10:08.600
The median gross return is strictly zero, which means the median profit is negative $200.

10:09.180 --> 10:10.520
They lost their entire entry fee.

10:10.520 --> 10:14.320
It doesn't matter how astronomical the payout is for the winners.

10:14.820 --> 10:19.520
I mean, the successful 600 people could make a million dollars each instead of 1,500.

10:19.780 --> 10:24.940
That massive payout would pull the mean, the average return, way up into highly profitable territory.

10:25.400 --> 10:32.020
Yeah, the platform could publish a glowing economic impact report stating that the average user generates a massive surplus.

10:32.460 --> 10:34.800
But the median user is still bleeding out.

10:34.800 --> 10:41.460
This explains the complete disconnect between corporate press releases and the reality of developer forums, doesn't it?

10:41.640 --> 10:42.900
It perfectly explains it.

10:43.360 --> 10:48.280
The platform releases a report touting billions of dollars in ecosystem revenue, pointing to the mean.

10:48.940 --> 10:53.100
Meanwhile, the forums are filled with thousands of creators complaining they can't afford to pay rent.

10:53.100 --> 10:59.360
Both the corporate PR team and the struggling creators are citing factual statistical realities.

10:59.980 --> 11:03.120
They're just describing different parts of a heavy-tailed distribution.

11:03.540 --> 11:09.660
As long as the probability of success is below 50%, the typical person is guaranteed to lose their entire investment.

11:09.660 --> 11:12.400
Here's where it gets really interesting.

11:13.220 --> 11:16.100
You could have a platform where the platform is highly profitable.

11:16.620 --> 11:20.620
The average return of a user is actually positive because of a few unicorns.

11:20.980 --> 11:26.600
But if I, the listener, pick a user at random, they are almost guaranteed to be in the red.

11:26.720 --> 11:28.060
That's the paradox in a nutshell.

11:28.460 --> 11:32.900
It's like a lottery where the jackpot is so big it mathematically justifies buying a ticket.

11:33.220 --> 11:36.120
But the median ticket is still just a piece of trash.

11:36.120 --> 11:40.700
The heavy-tailed distribution completely masks the reality of the median experience.

11:40.860 --> 11:45.060
But, you know, the paper doesn't just stop at diagnosing this disparity as a negative phenomenon.

11:45.380 --> 11:45.460
No.

11:45.660 --> 11:53.760
It introduces a concept called informational externalities, which complicates how we judge the overall value of the platform to the wider world.

11:54.680 --> 11:55.980
Informational externalities.

11:56.620 --> 12:01.600
Okay, so even if thousands of people are losing their money, you're saying there is a hidden benefit?

12:01.600 --> 12:06.420
Where is the upside to 9,000 people failing to build a successful app?

12:07.280 --> 12:10.500
Well, the upside exists for everyone who didn't build the app.

12:11.320 --> 12:11.700
How so?

12:12.260 --> 12:18.040
When a participant undertakes an experiment and fails, they suffer a private financial loss, right?

12:18.500 --> 12:21.060
But that failure generates information.

12:22.040 --> 12:22.480
Oh, I see.

12:22.960 --> 12:27.720
The rest of the market watches that failure and learns what features don't resonate with users.

12:27.720 --> 12:28.280
Exactly.

12:29.380 --> 12:33.800
They learn what marketing strategies fall flat or what technology stacks are unstable.

12:35.040 --> 12:38.860
So the failure of the individual becomes the free education of the collective.

12:39.560 --> 12:44.840
The market absorbs the data without having to pay the $200 entry fee or spend the time writing the code.

12:45.060 --> 12:47.440
That's actually incredibly efficient for society as a whole.

12:47.440 --> 12:48.080
Right.

12:48.600 --> 13:00.580
Economists define social surplus as the sum of the financial output, the private, non-financial value to the users, and this external informational benefit, minus the actual cost of resources.

13:01.000 --> 13:10.280
So a privately unprofitable experiment can be immensely socially valuable if the information it generates prevents future waste.

13:10.280 --> 13:13.280
Or if it points the industry in a better direction.

13:14.020 --> 13:22.060
Society is effectively outsourcing the immense cost of discovering bad ideas to a massive pool of enthusiastic, failing users.

13:22.840 --> 13:27.760
And the platform acts as a highly efficient engine for extracting those failed experiments.

13:28.260 --> 13:30.960
It coordinates the trial and error on a massive scale.

13:31.420 --> 13:38.720
It privatizes the financial gains to the platform and the few winners, while socializing the informational gains to the rest of the industry.

13:38.720 --> 13:43.620
Okay, this brings us to the million-dollar question of this entire deep dive.

13:44.620 --> 13:54.920
If the typical user is losing their shirt, and the average user is losing money, and society is just harvesting their failures for data, why on earth do people keep signing up?

13:55.380 --> 13:56.660
That is the big question.

13:57.240 --> 14:00.680
Are 10,000 people just being tricked every single month?

14:00.720 --> 14:01.480
Are they being deceived?

14:02.140 --> 14:04.060
That is the natural assumption, right?

14:04.060 --> 14:11.200
We want to believe that widespread financial loss must be the result of fraud or systemic irrationality.

14:11.540 --> 14:13.080
Because who signs up to lose money?

14:13.640 --> 14:20.160
But Flyxion's paper explicitly demonstrates that this model requires neither deception nor a lack of rationality from the users.

14:20.520 --> 14:20.940
Wait, really?

14:21.400 --> 14:28.460
You are telling me it is rational to enter a game where you know your expected financial return is negative $110?

14:29.160 --> 14:29.900
It is.

14:30.140 --> 14:32.700
That sounds like the literal definition of a gambling addiction.

14:32.700 --> 14:39.700
It only looks like a gambling addiction if we assume humans operate as cold, calculating, profit-maximization machines.

14:40.340 --> 14:40.980
Which we don't.

14:41.160 --> 14:41.620
Exactly.

14:42.360 --> 14:45.140
The paper introduces a more comprehensive utility equation.

14:45.800 --> 14:49.920
A participant's utility isn't just their expected financial return minus the cost.

14:50.440 --> 14:53.120
It includes two massive non-financial variables.

14:53.800 --> 14:54.280
What are they?

14:54.680 --> 14:56.440
Consumption value and learning value.

14:56.720 --> 14:58.380
Okay, let's look at consumption value.

14:59.180 --> 15:01.800
I imagine this isn't about consuming a physical product.

15:01.800 --> 15:08.160
Consumption value is the immediate, intrinsic psychological reward you get from just making the attempt.

15:08.880 --> 15:09.880
It is the aspiration.

15:10.420 --> 15:11.520
The aspiration, right.

15:11.640 --> 15:20.940
It is the joy of the build, the thrill of pursuing a passion project, or the powerful identity boost of being able to call yourself a founder or a creator.

15:21.440 --> 15:23.560
It's the lifestyle branding of tech.

15:23.840 --> 15:26.260
People pay for the feeling of being in the arena.

15:26.960 --> 15:28.200
And what about learning value?

15:28.200 --> 15:30.280
Learning value is instrumental.

15:30.840 --> 15:36.780
It is the transferable skill or knowledge you gain from the attempt itself, regardless of the financial outcome.

15:37.200 --> 15:42.780
Okay, so even if your mobile game gets zero downloads and you lose your $200, you learned something.

15:43.320 --> 15:43.600
Right.

15:43.700 --> 15:45.340
You learned how to code in a new language.

15:45.620 --> 15:48.200
You learned how to navigate an app store submission process.

15:48.200 --> 15:50.500
And you learned how to run a basic ad campaign.

15:51.260 --> 15:54.200
That knowledge fundamentally changes your baseline competence for the future.

15:55.160 --> 15:58.560
It increases your expected financial return for your next attempt.

15:58.840 --> 16:01.980
So if we put the equation together, it makes perfect sense.

16:02.240 --> 16:02.780
Let's hear it.

16:02.780 --> 16:18.520
If your consumption value, the sheer joy of trying, plus your learning value, the skills you acquire, adds up to more than the $110 you expect to lose, then paying that $200 fee is a perfectly rational, utility-maximizing decision.

16:18.520 --> 16:24.140
You are rationally paying for an experience and an education, not an investment vehicle.

16:24.300 --> 16:24.700
Exactly.

16:25.260 --> 16:34.580
If you've ever paid a monthly fee for a coding boot camp or a website builder and quietly canceled it a year later with nothing to show for it financially, you might feel like you were tricked.

16:35.060 --> 16:37.640
But mathematically, you just bought a hobby.

16:38.020 --> 16:44.780
Think about someone paying for a luxury vacation or a university course in a subject they love but won't pursue professionally.

16:44.780 --> 16:49.220
Right. No one expects a vacation to yield a positive financial ROI.

16:49.900 --> 16:52.940
You pay for it because the consumption value is high.

16:53.620 --> 17:00.880
The digital platform is essentially monetizing that exact same aspiration but wrapping it in the aesthetic of a business venture.

17:01.620 --> 17:04.140
So what does this all mean for the platform's marketing?

17:04.460 --> 17:08.540
Are they selling a business solution or are they really just selling a hobby?

17:09.160 --> 17:13.360
It feels like selling a gym membership to someone you know will only come for two weeks.

17:13.360 --> 17:17.840
The gym analogy perfectly captures the economics of aspiration here.

17:18.320 --> 17:24.520
They get the aspiration value of buying the membership even if they don't get the financial or physical return.

17:25.020 --> 17:29.980
The gym knows full well that 90% of the people signing up are going to stop coming by February.

17:30.700 --> 17:34.720
The gym only has enough treadmills for a tiny fraction of its total member base.

17:35.200 --> 17:37.220
But the people signing up aren't being scammed.

17:37.600 --> 17:40.280
They are buying the feeling of taking control of their health.

17:40.280 --> 17:42.900
They get the aspiration value the moment they swipe their card.

17:43.560 --> 17:48.720
The platform is relying on a massive pool of users whose primary compensation is psychological.

17:49.660 --> 17:55.660
And the paper models this behavior using the concept of the hazard rate of the non-financial value distribution.

17:56.500 --> 17:56.880
Hazard rate.

17:57.540 --> 17:58.800
That sounds incredibly dense.

17:59.360 --> 18:02.520
How does this actually map to human behavior in our digital room?

18:02.520 --> 18:07.500
Think of the hazard rate as a measure of how many people are sitting exactly on the fence.

18:07.620 --> 18:07.740
Okay.

18:08.380 --> 18:10.540
You have a distribution of potential users in the world.

18:11.400 --> 18:13.700
On one extreme you have diehard enthusiasts.

18:14.220 --> 18:20.660
These people have such immense aspiration and consumption value that they would pay $1,000 just for the thrill of trying.

18:21.020 --> 18:23.520
The $200 price tag is irrelevant to them.

18:23.720 --> 18:24.920
They are already on the platform.

18:25.260 --> 18:26.520
They're the guaranteed revenue.

18:26.520 --> 18:32.180
On the other extreme you have people with zero interest in building an app who wouldn't try it even if it were free.

18:32.440 --> 18:32.680
Makes sense.

18:33.240 --> 18:35.260
But in the middle you have the fence sitters.

18:36.020 --> 18:41.680
Their combined aspiration and learning value is hovering right around the $110 expected loss mark.

18:42.240 --> 18:46.680
Ah, so the hazard rate measures the density of the population right at that borderline.

18:47.060 --> 18:47.440
Exactly.

18:47.680 --> 18:53.720
So if the hazard rate is high, it means you have a massive crowd of people who are just barely talking themselves out of signing up.

18:53.720 --> 18:58.960
And if the platform drops its price by just a few dollars, that massive crowd suddenly says,

18:59.140 --> 19:02.420
okay, now it's cheap enough that my curiosity justifies the cost.

19:03.000 --> 19:05.960
A high hazard rate means participation is highly elastic.

19:06.580 --> 19:09.160
Small price changes trigger massive floods of new users.

19:09.500 --> 19:12.020
This sets up a fascinating dilemma for the platform.

19:12.620 --> 19:15.340
I mean, if tools get cheaper, who actually benefits?

19:15.620 --> 19:16.300
It's a great question.

19:16.740 --> 19:18.640
The paper actually has a caveat about this.

19:18.640 --> 19:22.920
If an open source movement releases a free tool that makes building apps easier,

19:23.060 --> 19:29.260
the platform running the app store suddenly gets thousands of new developers submitting projects without having to lower its own fees.

19:29.640 --> 19:32.740
The platform absorbs the windfall of the lowered barrier to entry.

19:33.020 --> 19:33.960
They get users for free.

19:34.260 --> 19:40.800
But if the platform decides to lower its own price, say dropping the entry fee from $200 to $100,

19:41.320 --> 19:44.300
it faces a brutal volume versus margin calculation.

19:44.300 --> 19:47.740
Right, because they aren't just giving a discount to the new people.

19:48.240 --> 19:54.040
They are losing $100 of pure profit on all the diehard enthusiasts who were perfectly happy paying $200.

19:54.420 --> 20:03.400
So the influx of new, highly sensitive elastic fence sitters has to be massive enough to offset the revenue lost from the inelastic enthusiasts.

20:03.560 --> 20:03.960
Exactly.

20:04.740 --> 20:08.660
But let's look at the result of that price drop from the perspective of the ecosystem itself.

20:08.660 --> 20:14.620
Okay, because the common wisdom in tech is that lowering the barrier to entry democratizes innovation.

20:15.340 --> 20:19.180
If you make it cheaper to try, you get a massive wave of new participants,

20:19.480 --> 20:23.700
which should lead to a massive renaissance of successful new projects.

20:24.020 --> 20:26.920
The idea that a rising tide lifts all boats.

20:27.300 --> 20:27.540
Right.

20:28.220 --> 20:33.260
But since lowering the price brings in more users motivated by non-financial aspiration,

20:33.860 --> 20:36.820
what does that do to the actual success rate on the platform?

20:36.820 --> 20:41.000
Well, the paper proves mathematically that in these specific environments,

20:41.220 --> 20:44.540
the idea of a rising tide is entirely false.

20:44.860 --> 20:48.060
It introduces the concept of sub-proportional successes.

20:48.580 --> 20:49.660
Sub-proportional.

20:50.060 --> 20:52.940
Meaning the number of winners doesn't scale with the number of attempts.

20:53.520 --> 20:54.500
But why wouldn't it?

20:55.020 --> 20:57.240
If I double the number of people swinging the bat,

20:57.740 --> 20:59.480
shouldn't I double the number of home runs?

20:59.980 --> 21:02.840
Not if the new people swinging the bat don't know how to hold it.

21:03.060 --> 21:03.380
Ouch.

21:04.340 --> 21:06.460
This is driven by a selection effect.

21:06.460 --> 21:10.060
We have to look at who was already on the platform when the price was high

21:10.060 --> 21:12.480
and who is being brought in by the discount.

21:12.800 --> 21:12.980
Okay.

21:13.160 --> 21:13.960
Let's profile them.

21:14.260 --> 21:17.240
The people paying the high price of $200 are likely the pros.

21:17.720 --> 21:19.320
They have a high probability of success.

21:19.680 --> 21:23.180
Maybe they're at 15% or 20% instead of 6%.

21:23.180 --> 21:23.720
Right.

21:23.820 --> 21:26.740
Because their expected financial return is so much higher,

21:27.280 --> 21:31.940
they don't need to rely on the psychological aspiration value to justify the cost.

21:32.100 --> 21:33.520
They expect to make money.

21:33.520 --> 21:37.060
So the highly capable participants are already in the room.

21:37.120 --> 21:40.320
They self-select into the platform even when it's expensive.

21:41.020 --> 21:43.500
And when you lower the price to attract the fence sitters,

21:43.700 --> 21:46.380
you are scraping the margin of the talent pool.

21:46.780 --> 21:51.160
You are attracting the people whose actual competence and success probability are much lower.

21:51.160 --> 21:56.440
These new users can only justify entering the market because the price finally dropped low enough

21:56.440 --> 21:58.880
that their pure aspiration could cover the gap.

21:58.880 --> 22:01.700
This is formalized in Lemma 1 of the paper.

22:02.260 --> 22:05.860
Under standard conditions, what they call increasing hazard rates,

22:06.400 --> 22:12.100
the marginal entrants brought in by a lower price have lower success probabilities than the existing average.

22:12.100 --> 22:16.720
So the new wave of users literally dilutes the overall talent pool.

22:17.420 --> 22:21.440
Therefore, a proportional increase in attempts, say 20% more users,

22:21.660 --> 22:26.800
leads to a less than proportional increase in successes, maybe only 5% more winners.

22:27.500 --> 22:29.200
Okay, so just to clarify for the listener,

22:29.880 --> 22:31.860
if a platform cuts its fees in half,

22:32.240 --> 22:35.380
we shouldn't expect twice as many successful businesses to be born.

22:35.800 --> 22:39.920
We'll just get a massive flood of enthusiastic amateurs diluting the pool.

22:39.920 --> 22:45.100
The success rate of the platform as a whole objectively drops when the platform gets cheaper.

22:45.940 --> 22:48.060
The ecosystem becomes flooded with noise,

22:48.260 --> 22:51.240
which makes it even harder for the capable users to find an audience.

22:51.980 --> 22:55.280
You see this constantly in digital storefronts and content platforms.

22:55.780 --> 22:59.300
When a platform makes it essentially free to publish an e-book,

22:59.820 --> 23:02.780
the total volume of content skyrockets into the millions.

23:03.240 --> 23:06.900
But the number of creators actually making a living wage barely moves.

23:06.900 --> 23:10.180
The platform monetizes the aspiration of the millions,

23:10.460 --> 23:13.040
while the overall quality of the average upload plummets.

23:13.900 --> 23:15.920
But I should note the paper's warning here.

23:16.440 --> 23:18.480
This is actually an empirical question.

23:18.820 --> 23:19.200
What do you mean?

23:19.700 --> 23:24.400
The model assumes that your competence and your non-financial aspiration are independent variables.

23:25.140 --> 23:26.800
But what if they are negatively dependent?

23:27.240 --> 23:28.120
Negative dependence.

23:28.920 --> 23:30.560
What does that look like in a human being?

23:30.560 --> 23:36.040
It means the people with the highest capability actually have the lowest aspiration to be on the platform.

23:36.580 --> 23:39.700
Usually because they have incredibly lucrative outside options.

23:40.020 --> 23:40.180
Ah.

23:40.960 --> 23:42.420
Like a brilliant software engineer.

23:42.840 --> 23:43.320
Exactly.

23:43.760 --> 23:46.840
They have the capability to build a highly successful app,

23:46.960 --> 23:51.340
but they also have the option to take a guaranteed, high-paying job at a major tech firm.

23:51.680 --> 23:55.480
So the genius needs to be financially bribed to come play on the platform,

23:55.960 --> 23:59.980
whereas the amateur has no lucrative outside options and is just doing it for the dream.

23:59.980 --> 24:03.720
If that negative dependence is strong enough, this rule could flip.

24:04.540 --> 24:08.480
Lowering the price might suddenly draw in highly capable people who are holding out.

24:08.780 --> 24:13.660
But assuming independence, cheaper experimentation objectively dilutes the winner pool.

24:14.280 --> 24:14.800
Exactly.

24:15.220 --> 24:18.600
So up to this point, we have been analyzing the very first attempt,

24:18.940 --> 24:21.520
the moment someone swipes their credit card for the first time.

24:22.320 --> 24:24.900
But platforms survive on recurring revenue.

24:25.640 --> 24:28.420
What happens after the user's first success or failure?

24:28.420 --> 24:31.800
This is where the structural dynamics get truly fascinating,

24:31.980 --> 24:34.600
because it dictates the long-term composition of the platform.

24:35.260 --> 24:39.300
The paper introduces a one-step memory continuation model.

24:39.520 --> 24:40.860
One-step memory.

24:41.240 --> 24:47.360
It divides all services into two broad categories based on how a user behaves after they experience an outcome.

24:48.120 --> 24:52.260
Expansion-oriented platforms and completion-oriented platforms.

24:52.260 --> 24:54.980
Okay, let's tackle expansion first.

24:55.900 --> 25:00.260
An expansion-oriented service is one where success encourages further purchases.

25:00.860 --> 25:05.340
The continuation rate after a success is higher than the continuation rate after a failure.

25:05.580 --> 25:08.940
This maps perfectly to cloud computing platforms like AWS.

25:09.640 --> 25:14.220
If I launch an app and the app goes incredibly viral, I don't cancel my server hosting.

25:14.320 --> 25:16.460
I frantically buy more server space.

25:16.460 --> 25:20.060
Right. Your success forces you to expand your usage.

25:20.380 --> 25:23.960
If your app fails, you shut the servers down and stop paying.

25:24.520 --> 25:29.840
In an expansion-oriented model, the platform's financial incentives are perfectly aligned with the user's success.

25:30.260 --> 25:32.640
With the more money I make, the more money they make.

25:32.740 --> 25:36.640
That is the symbiotic relationship we all assume exists everywhere.

25:37.200 --> 25:41.260
But then we have the second category, the completion-oriented service.

25:41.560 --> 25:42.160
Now what's that?

25:42.160 --> 25:45.100
In this model, success terminates demand.

25:45.920 --> 25:50.660
The continuation rate after a failure is actually higher than the continuation rate after a success.

25:50.840 --> 25:51.500
This is wild.

25:51.880 --> 25:56.820
It's the difference between a cloud hosting service versus a test prep software.

25:57.340 --> 25:57.800
Exactly.

25:58.200 --> 26:00.960
Test prep software or specific certification courses.

26:01.480 --> 26:03.680
If I pass the bar exam, I delete the app.

26:03.840 --> 26:04.220
I'm done.

26:04.540 --> 26:08.720
But if I fail, I renew my subscription for another six months to try again.

26:08.720 --> 26:14.340
The commercial interests of a completion-oriented platform inherently diverges from the progress of its customer.

26:14.780 --> 26:17.540
They literally make more money when you fail and try again.

26:18.160 --> 26:18.640
They do.

26:19.320 --> 26:22.600
And over time, this changes the composition of the active users.

26:23.020 --> 26:23.420
How so?

26:23.800 --> 26:24.420
Think about it.

26:24.660 --> 26:30.820
As the months roll by, the highly capable users, the fast learners, achieve their goals and exit the platform.

26:31.220 --> 26:34.240
The unsuccessful users fail their goals and renew.

26:34.240 --> 26:41.580
So, month by month, the platform is automatically purging its most capable users and retaining its least capable users.

26:41.980 --> 26:47.480
Over time, the active user base slowly concentrates into a dense pool of low success types.

26:48.080 --> 26:54.440
This causes the platform's overall success rate to drop over time, even with no changes in technology or individual competence.

26:54.840 --> 27:01.800
So, for completion-oriented services, their most persistent, high-lifetime-value customers are their absolute worst performers.

27:01.800 --> 27:08.760
Exactly. And this completely shatters traditional business logic, especially when it comes to pricing elasticity.

27:09.460 --> 27:10.780
Let's run a hypothetical on that.

27:11.420 --> 27:14.740
The platform raises its monthly subscription fee by 20%.

27:14.740 --> 27:23.020
Well, in an expansion-oriented model like the cloud host, raising prices disproportionately kicks out your worst, least valuable users.

27:23.600 --> 27:25.460
The viral apps will just pay the higher fee.

27:25.580 --> 27:26.260
The clean house.

27:26.260 --> 27:33.800
Right. But if failures continue, more like in the bar exam software, raising prices kicks out the successful people.

27:34.140 --> 27:36.280
Because the persistent failures are living on the margin.

27:36.900 --> 27:40.320
Their utility is propped up entirely by non-financial aspiration.

27:40.740 --> 27:42.300
They are incredibly sensitive to price.

27:42.780 --> 27:46.300
So, a price hike leaves you reliant on the persistent failures.

27:46.300 --> 27:54.120
This completely shatters the idea that the customer's always right translates to customer success equals platform revenue.

27:54.640 --> 27:59.560
We have to pivot now to what I think is the most cutting-edge application of this entire framework in the paper.

28:00.600 --> 28:02.760
Computation sold by usage.

28:03.140 --> 28:06.320
Specifically, generative AI and language model inference.

28:06.740 --> 28:10.040
Yes. This is where it gets very real for anyone using the internet today.

28:10.040 --> 28:15.520
When an AI generates an answer, we measure that computation in units called tokens.

28:15.900 --> 28:21.500
Right. And quality increases with computation, but with diminishing returns, right? It's concave.

28:21.680 --> 28:22.240
Exactly.

28:22.780 --> 28:24.960
The first 50 words of a summary are crucial.

28:25.400 --> 28:26.960
The next 50 add context.

28:27.520 --> 28:32.280
But by the time the AI is generating its thousandth word, it's just rambling.

28:32.680 --> 28:35.040
The value of each additional word drops.

28:35.040 --> 28:45.360
So, the user's optimal stopping point is the exact moment where the expected improvement in quality exactly equals the cost of the next token of computation.

28:46.060 --> 28:53.440
If a token costs a tenth of a cent, I only want the AI to generate another word if that word makes the summary a tenth of a cent better.

28:53.760 --> 28:54.960
That is peak optimization.

28:55.560 --> 28:59.160
However, the platform makes a margin on every single token.

28:59.360 --> 29:00.100
The meter is running.

29:00.260 --> 29:03.640
This creates what the paper calls the local divergence proposition.

29:03.640 --> 29:09.620
Just past your optimal stopping point, forcing the AI to compute a little bit more hurts you slightly.

29:09.920 --> 29:11.180
It's a second order negative, right?

29:11.480 --> 29:13.240
Exactly. Second order negative for the user.

29:13.500 --> 29:15.800
But it helps the platform's revenue directly.

29:15.980 --> 29:17.840
It's a first order positive for them.

29:18.120 --> 29:18.260
Wow.

29:18.520 --> 29:23.180
If the provider controls the length of the output and is trying to maximize the single transaction,

29:23.440 --> 29:30.420
they're economically incentivized to push the computation all the way to the quantity control boundary.

29:30.780 --> 29:32.320
The quantity control boundary.

29:32.320 --> 29:38.820
They give you excess computation until you were just barely getting enough value to not quit the service entirely.

29:39.300 --> 29:45.900
Wait, so is this why AI sometimes gives me a massively long, overly wordy answer when I just ask the simple question?

29:46.080 --> 29:47.420
It is the exact reason.

29:47.640 --> 29:49.780
It's like a taxi driver taking the scenic route.

29:50.020 --> 29:50.780
The meter's running.

29:51.600 --> 29:55.180
But wouldn't the platform worry I'd eventually just cancel my account?

29:55.180 --> 29:58.360
The paper explicitly mentions retention.

29:58.920 --> 30:04.480
If pushing extra tokens hurts user retention to the point of cancellation, the platform might dial it back.

30:04.980 --> 30:05.740
They have to calibrate.

30:06.040 --> 30:08.620
Exactly. They find the optimal level of annoyance.

30:08.880 --> 30:09.320
Exactly.

30:09.860 --> 30:12.580
Also, the paper notes a fascinating sign flip here.

30:12.840 --> 30:13.800
What kind of flip?

30:13.800 --> 30:19.620
If the AI is sold via a fixed subscription, the provider's incentive reverses completely.

30:19.860 --> 30:25.620
Because their marginal revenue per transaction drops to zero, but their marginal costs are still real.

30:25.840 --> 30:26.140
Right.

30:26.660 --> 30:30.600
So they want the AI to do as little computation as possible to save costs.

30:31.060 --> 30:38.040
The exact same AI model will give you a clipped, bare-bones answer on a subscription and a massive essay if you pay per token.

30:38.340 --> 30:39.860
That is genuinely mind-bending.

30:40.240 --> 30:40.820
It gets better.

30:40.820 --> 30:44.160
The paper brings up an analogy to meritocracy.

30:44.900 --> 30:48.780
In human institutions, evaluators often reward visible reasoning or effort.

30:49.380 --> 30:51.500
We reward people who show their work.

30:51.840 --> 30:53.400
We reward the performance of labor.

30:53.780 --> 31:00.300
So if a system learns from human feedback to produce traces of reasoning that aren't actually causally necessary for the answer,

31:00.900 --> 31:04.760
it creates unnecessary computation that goes unnoticed.

31:05.120 --> 31:08.700
It deliberately confuses expenditure with true value.

31:08.700 --> 31:10.340
It fakes the sweat.

31:10.820 --> 31:16.120
And under a usage pricing model, the platform gets paid for every drop of that fake sweat.

31:16.520 --> 31:20.240
It's a profound alignment of human bias and platform revenue incentives.

31:20.760 --> 31:20.780
Okay.

31:20.900 --> 31:30.140
To wrap up this deep analysis, I have to ask, how could a listener or an economist actually prove these theories wrong in the real world?

31:30.700 --> 31:33.560
A model without falsifiability is just a philosophy.

31:33.560 --> 31:34.260
Absolutely.

31:34.260 --> 31:34.360
Absolutely.

31:34.860 --> 31:38.720
And the paper outlines specific refuting observations in section 12.

31:38.980 --> 31:39.460
Let's hear them.

31:39.840 --> 31:44.480
To falsify the three-way distinction between the mean, the median, and the platform profit,

31:44.780 --> 31:50.120
you just need to measure a market and find that the median gross return actually exceeds the price of entry.

31:50.120 --> 31:55.980
So if the median user actually makes a net profit, then typical users aren't losing money and the model breaks.

31:56.060 --> 31:56.420
Exactly.

31:57.160 --> 31:59.160
What about testing the selection effect?

31:59.660 --> 32:01.600
The idea that cheaper tools dilute the pool.

32:01.760 --> 32:03.020
You have to randomize prices.

32:03.520 --> 32:05.920
You A-B test the entry price for a massive cohort.

32:05.920 --> 32:08.880
Because we can't just look at chronological cohorts, right?

32:09.040 --> 32:10.760
Technology and the market change over time.

32:11.060 --> 32:11.140
Right.

32:11.300 --> 32:12.600
You need independent measurements.

32:13.360 --> 32:18.480
If the new entrants drawn in by a cheaper price have an equal or higher success rate than the baseline,

32:18.640 --> 32:21.640
the sub-proportional success lemma is broken.

32:22.060 --> 32:23.520
And what about the aspirational stuff?

32:23.860 --> 32:28.700
You have to measure the distribution of non-financial values separately from the entry decisions

32:28.700 --> 32:30.860
through willingness to pay elicitation.

32:31.260 --> 32:31.540
Okay.

32:31.720 --> 32:34.220
So we can't just say, look, the platform is rich.

32:34.220 --> 32:35.880
Therefore, users are being fleeced.

32:36.500 --> 32:41.700
We have to do the hard work of separating out the actual financial return from the aspiration value.

32:42.160 --> 32:44.780
We have to separate the dream from the balance sheet.

32:44.960 --> 32:45.240
We do.

32:45.820 --> 32:50.320
Until we quantify the non-financial utility, we are only looking at half the equation.

32:50.760 --> 32:53.160
Well, I want to thank you for joining this deep dive.

32:53.500 --> 32:59.140
To summarize, platform profitability is an elegant dance of entry, selection, learning, and continuation,

32:59.840 --> 33:02.200
none of which guarantees financial success for the user.

33:02.660 --> 33:04.180
It's a complex system for sure.

33:04.180 --> 33:09.040
But I want to leave you, the listener, with the final thought to mull over or explore on your own.

33:09.940 --> 33:14.280
We've spent this hour looking at the math of why users fail while platforms thrive.

33:14.280 --> 33:15.820
But think about this.

33:15.820 --> 33:22.760
What if the ultimate, most beneficial platforms for human progress are designed to operate exactly this way?

33:22.760 --> 33:24.260
That's an interesting framing.

33:24.620 --> 33:31.280
If society benefits from the information created by thousands of failed experiments, informational externalities,

33:31.720 --> 33:35.540
then perhaps the ideal innovation engine isn't one where everyone succeeds.

33:35.800 --> 33:37.360
It's one that harvests failure.

33:37.700 --> 33:38.100
Exactly.

33:38.100 --> 33:44.580
Perhaps it's one where the platform is highly efficient at getting millions of us to cheerfully pay for the privilege of failing

33:44.580 --> 33:47.720
so that the rest of humanity can reap the knowledge of our mistakes.

33:48.560 --> 33:52.020
Next time you pay a subscription fee for a project that doesn't pan out,

33:52.600 --> 33:55.080
ask yourself, who just benefited from your failure?

33:55.080 --> 34:09.400
If you have any questions, take it away from, or do not change the technology to report that or use any other.

34:09.400 --> 34:11.120
Now, I'll pick a top issue here.

34:11.120 --> 34:11.980
So, keep playing moving through any questions.

34:14.120 --> 34:15.180
If you don't have time to talk before financial advice,

34:15.180 --> 34:16.540
I'll pick up before you stand up and pick it up again.

34:16.540 --> 34:18.180
If you do not answer what happens, perhaps I'll pick up after this.

